@meta
id: 10-23
title: Capstone — sniping inside one line
chapter: 10
type: scenario
skills: obj-quote, inner-vs-around, find-f, find-t, op-motion
par: 62
minutes: 6
vscode: clicking exactly where you want
open: query.ts

@brief
w and b move by word, which is often too coarse. To land on an exact
character, use f (find). fx puts the cursor on the next x on this
line. It is the single biggest speed-up available on a long line.

f and t combine with operators, so  dt"  means "delete up to the
next quote" and  ct,  means "change up to the next comma".

In query.ts:

  1. "PENDING"  ->  "SHIPPED"
  2. limit: 50  ->  limit: 25
  3. delete the  phone,  field from the csv string
  4. "Order #1042 - awaiting payment - do not ship"
       ->  "Order #1042 - ready to ship"

No arrow keys. Aim with f.

@keys
fx      jump forward to the next x on this line  (F = backwards)
tx      jump forward to just BEFORE the next x   (T = backwards)
;  ,    repeat that jump forwards / backwards
3fx     jump to the third x
dfx     delete up to and including x
dtx     delete up to but not including x
ctx     change up to but not including x
ci"     change inside quotes - still the fastest way for step 1

@hints
Step 1 is a text object job: put the cursor before PENDING, then
f" to land on its opening quote, then ci" .
--
Step 2: f5 lands on the 5 of 50. Then  r2  replaces just that one
character with a 2. r is "replace one character and stay put".
--
Step 3: get to the p of phone, then  df,  deletes through the comma
that follows it - field and separator gone in one move.
--
Step 4: land on the a of "awaiting", then  ct"  changes everything up
to the closing quote. Type  ready to ship  and Esc.

@solution
Line 1:  f" ;  ci" SHIPPED Esc        ( ; repeats the f jump )
         f5 r2

Line 2:  j  fp  df,

Line 3:  j  fa  ct" ready to ship Esc

:wq

f then ; is the rhythm to build. "Next quote... no, the one after
that" is f" ; and not a single arrow press.

@start query.ts
const url = buildUrl(host, port, "/orders", { status: "PENDING", limit: 50, cursor: null });
const csv = "id,name,email,phone,country,created";
const label = "Order #1042 - awaiting payment - do not ship";

@expected query.ts
const url = buildUrl(host, port, "/orders", { status: "SHIPPED", limit: 25, cursor: null });
const csv = "id,name,email,country,created";
const label = "Order #1042 - ready to ship";
